**Proof.**

First, note that $G$ is nonempty (otherwise the claim is vacuous). Pick any $f \in G$. By property (b), $f^{-1} \in G$, and by property (a), $f \circ f^{-1} = \mathrm{id} \in G$. So the identity belongs to $G$.

**Representing elements by fixed point and slope.** Every $f \in G$ has the form $f(x) = ax + b$ with $a \neq 0$ (non-constant). If $a = 1$, then $f(x) = x + b$ has a fixed point only when $b = 0$, i.e., $f = \mathrm{id}$. Thus every **non-identity** element of $G$ has $a \neq 1$ and a **unique** fixed point $p_f = b/(1-a)$. We rewrite such $f$ as
$$f(x) = p_f + a(x - p_f).$$
The inverse is $f^{-1}(x) = p_f + \tfrac{1}{a}(x - p_f)$, which has the **same** fixed point $p_f$.

**Key computation: the commutator is a translation.** Let $f, g \in G$ be non-identity elements, written as
$$f(x) = p + a(x-p), \qquad g(x) = q + c(x-q),$$
with $a, c \neq 0, 1$ and fixed points $p, q$ respectively. Consider the commutator
$$h := f \circ g \circ f^{-1} \circ g^{-1} \in G$$
(which lies in $G$ by repeated application of (a) and (b)). We compute $h(x)$ step by step.

- $g^{-1}(x) = q + \tfrac{1}{c}(x - q) = \dfrac{x - q}{c} + q$.
- $f^{-1}(g^{-1}(x)) = p + \dfrac{1}{a}\!\left(\dfrac{x-q}{c} + q - p\right) = \dfrac{x + q(c-1) + cp(a-1)}{ca}$.
- $g\!\left(f^{-1}(g^{-1}(x))\right) = q + c\!\left(\dfrac{x + q(c-1) + cp(a-1)}{ca} - q\right) = \dfrac{x + (a-1)\bigl(cp - q(c-1)\bigr)}{a}$, using $c - 1 - ca + a = -(c-1)(a-1)$.
- $h(x) = f\!\left(g(f^{-1}(g^{-1}(x)))\right) = p + a\!\left(\dfrac{x + (a-1)(cp - q(c-1))}{a} - p\right)$
$$= x + (a-1)\bigl(cp - q(c-1)\bigr) - (a-1)p = x + (a-1)\bigl(p(c-1) - q(c-1)\bigr).$$

Therefore
$$\boxed{h(x) = x + (a-1)(c-1)(p - q).}$$

**Applying property (c).** The map $h$ is a translation $x \mapsto x + t$ with $t = (a-1)(c-1)(p-q)$. A translation has a fixed point if and only if $t = 0$. Since $h \in G$, property (c) forces $h$ to have a fixed point, so
$$(a-1)(c-1)(p - q) = 0.$$

Since $f$ and $g$ are both non-identity, $a \neq 1$ and $c \neq 1$, hence $(a-1)(c-1) \neq 0$. Therefore
$$p = q.$$

**Conclusion.** Any two non-identity elements of $G$ have the same fixed point. Call this common value $k$. The identity also satisfies $\mathrm{id}(k) = k$. Hence
$$f(k) = k \quad \text{for all } f \in G. \qquad \blacksquare$$

